o end a  pregnancy piercing  is a gynecologist in wut he says the  concerns about the register are  unjustified as the data is available  anyway but he also says he is fed up  with the issue being politicized in  poland  we as doctors would like to be left  alone by politicians we want them to  stop using us for their purposes both  sides are doing it  piotr shiroshevsky fears the restrictive  abortion rules in poland will have  lasting consequences  there are women who now are afraid to  get pregnant  they think what am i going to do when i  find out my fetus has an illness or gene  damage  that's why they decide not to get  pregnant at all  others skip prenatal examinations  because they fear the results  but the debate in poland continues with  pro-life activists demanding further  restrictions and criminal charges  against people selling abortion pills  the activists who sell the pills  earn money on that and this is what we  want to stop we want to stop this  business of  business of death you could say  claudia kustup cannot come to terms with  what's going on in poland she's hoping  for a political change soon otherwise  she says she's going to leave the  country  well for more let's cross over to our  correspondent mike elena  in warsaw magdalena i understand polish  lawmakers are working on further  measures related to pregnancies and  abortion tell us what's in the pipeline  well the government wants to establish a  central register including the  information about pregnancy which is as  they say just you know to provide the  best medical care um for instance in  cases of emergency but you know it  became a source of a suspicion of course  and uh since the abortion and abortion  law in poland become as such a disputed  area what i want to say is you know  maybe in other times and other countries  that would not be such a big topic  as it became now in poland when of  course there is a lot of inconvenience  what for the register is being planned  rights activists in poland have staged  multiple street protests against tougher  abortion laws have these protests had  any impact on lawmakers  well i don't think so indeed because  there has been so many decisions made in  uh last month in poland by the ruling  party law and justice and there have  been a lot of protests in several cases  and  they did not indeed change the decisions  of the government but just to be  precisely you know there has been even  an idea to tighten um the law which we  have now in poland and the ruling party  was as  the majority of the parliament against  it so um it also shows that they might  maybe not be any further step in the  abortion  law itself so where do you think things  will go from here in poland's abortion  debate given that the country already  does have very restrictive laws in place  yes it's hard to imagine that it could  be even more restricted it is now you  know uh abortion is just legal as it was  said in the report in two cases rape  incest or or um the threatened health of  the mother um so this is already a very  restrictive things and of course there  is a debate going on  among doctors and in hospitals there has  been also a hospital in the eastern part  and of poland  which  declared that  doctors are hesitating  of some steps because they are afraid  of what might happen to them so there is  a big debate going on in this country  and we never know how it ends because  the society is very strongly divided  also in that case in poland  magdalena thank you very much that was  dw's magdalene  in warsaw  you
 

